title: "Decoding a coffee scale's BLE protocol without an SDK"
slug: "timemore-dot-ble"
date: "2026-10-31"
tags: [android, bluetooth, ble, reverse-engineering, kotlin]
description: "Reverse-engineering the Timemore Dot coffee scale's BLE protocol from captures: GATT from zero, an A5 5A frame with a CRC you only half-trust, and recorded sessions replayed by a test."
---

My Timemore Dot coffee scale has no screen or SDK. I decoded its BLE traffic to read the weight and cross-checked the result against [Timemore's official protocol documentation](https://github.com/TIMEMORE-COFFEE/open-scale-protocol), which also covers the Dot.

## BLE for people who never touched it

This scale acts as a GATT server: it exposes a table of services, each holding characteristics, each optionally with descriptors. The phone is the client. You call `connectGatt()` to get a `BluetoothGatt`, `discoverServices()` to see the table, and then you read characteristics, write them, or subscribe to notifications.

Notifications are opt-in twice. You call `setCharacteristicNotification()` on the phone, and you write the Client Characteristic Configuration descriptor (`0x2902`) so the device knows to push. Data then shows up in `onCharacteristicChanged()`.

Bonding is `createBond()`: an OS-level pairing that stores a key and gives you link encryption, separate from GATT. The ATT MTU limits the size of an ATT PDU; the default of 23 bytes leaves 20 bytes for a notification value. `requestMtu()` starts an asynchronous negotiation: `onMtuChanged` reports the result, which may differ from the requested value. Starting with Android 14, the first request on a connection asks for 517 regardless of the requested value. The [Android BLE guide](https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/bluetooth/ble/connect-gatt-server) covers the rest.

This scale exposes one custom service, `fff0`, with two characteristics (alongside a Device Information Service): `fff1` notifies (scale to app) and `fff2` writes without response (app to scale).

## The handshake

The working order is:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant App
    participant Scale
    App->>Scale: connectGatt, retry up to 3x at 500 ms
    App->>Scale: requestMtu(247)
    App->>Scale: createBond
    App->>Scale: discoverServices
    App->>Scale: subscribe fff1 notifications
    Note over App: 500 ms
    App->>Scale: unit grams
    Note over App: 200 ms
    App->>Scale: standard mode, battery query
    Scale-->>App: weight notifications
```

On my unit, streaming required an encrypted link. It appeared to remember one bonded phone; attempts from another were associated with an HCI `0x3E` error, and holding the button until the LED blinked fast allowed re-pairing. I also encountered status 133 on connection after idle, so the app retries; I did not measure how often it happens. I request an MTU of 247 because real notifications carry several frames: the largest capture was 36 bytes, and 15 of 3,419 idle notifications exceed the 20-byte default, so they would have been split.

## Framing: `A5 5A`

Every frame looks like this:

```
A5 5A <type> <cmd> <len_hi> <len_lo> <payload…> <crc_hi> <crc_lo>
```

`type` is `01` for notification, `02` for read, `03` for write/ack. The length field is two bytes, big-endian.

Outgoing frames carry a CRC-16/MODBUS checksum (init `0xFFFF`, reflected polynomial `0xA001`) over every byte before it, big-endian. That big-endian order already deviates from the MODBUS convention, which sends the CRC low byte first, and you only notice by computing the value. The "set unit to grams" frame `A5 5A 03 06 00 01 00` checksums to `E8 A7`, so the app sends `A5 5A 03 06 00 01 00 E8 A7`.

Incoming frames are the opposite. Every one of the 3,931 received frames in my two real captures ends in `00 00`: the scale never fills in the checksum. For example, `A5 5A 01 01 00 09 00 00 01 DA 00 00 00 00 00` computes to `09 53`, but the received frame ends in `00 00`. Timemore calls these bytes reserved for device-to-app frames. The app therefore does not validate incoming CRCs on the tested firmware.

I compared the layout with the official protocol and checked the fields used by the app against captures.

## One notification is not one frame

A single notification can hold several frames (I saw an ack, a battery read, and a weight update packed into one 36-byte notification), and a frame could be split across notifications; the decoder handles that case in a test, although I did not find a split frame in these two captures. So the decoder's job is mostly resynchronization, not parsing.

The loop: append to a buffer, scan for `A5 5A`, read the declared length, drop anything above 64 as out of sync, wait for the whole frame, emit it, repeat. I keep a trailing `A5` because its `5A` might arrive in the next chunk. The buffer is capped at 512 bytes so a garbage stream cannot grow forever, and I reset it on reconnect.

## The payload

The `01 01` frame is the weight update, measured at 10.01 Hz on my unit. It is 9 bytes:

```kotlin
fun uint16(p: ByteArray, at: Int): Int =
    ((p[at].toInt() and 0xFF) shl 8) or (p[at + 1].toInt() and 0xFF)
fun int16(p: ByteArray, at: Int): Int = uint16(p, at).toShort().toInt()
fun int32(p: ByteArray, at: Int): Int = (uint16(p, at) shl 16) or uint16(p, at + 2)

fun decode(p: ByteArray) {
    require(p.size >= 8)
    val weightGrams = int32(p, 0) / 10.0
    val flowGramsPerSecond = int16(p, 4) / 10.0
    val timerSeconds = uint16(p, 6)
    val overload = p.size > 8 && p[8] != 0.toByte()
}
```

A real frame `00 00 04 DA 02 24 00 00 00` decodes to 124.2 g at 54.8 g/s with the timer at 0.

Battery is read with `02 05`, and it comes back as two bytes `[bars, percent]` (`03 64` is 3 bars, 100 %). The scale also pushes that payload on its own, but not on a reliable schedule, so the app polls every 60 seconds and takes the push whenever it shows up. Writes include `03 0D` tare, `03 02 [01|02|03]` timer start/pause/reset, `03 06 [00]` set grams and `03 08 [01 00]` standard mode. The capture contains acknowledgements `03 <cmd> 00 01 01` for the unit and mode writes. `02 13` is a read request, with model data in its reply.

## Trusting the numbers

The raw flow saturates on a sudden load (99.9 g/s on my unit), so I clamp it to 0–25 g/s and smooth it. That clamp is a decision in the app, not a limit of the scale, which reports higher. I found no stability flag in the decoded weight payload, so stability is computed in the app. That is where "just read the weight" turns into a data problem. The overload flag turned on around 1300 g on this unit, and an idle session stayed connected for 5 min 42 s with no keep-alive. Both of those are measurements on one device, not a datasheet.

I keep the recorded notifications as JSONL and replay them through the decoder in `RecordedSessionTest`, which asserts no unknown frames, the overload event, the unit and mode acknowledgements, and about 3,400 weight readings in the idle capture. What the app does with those readings after that is a whole other post.
